han others  but just because there might be some  echo in your room doesn't mean you  should find a different room to record  in it might still work  cover up hard surfaces like walls with  books  add a carpet to the floor or in my case  astroturf to absorb the sound  block any gaps to a larger room with a  thick material if you have some budget  to spare get some professional foam to  absorb the sound or if not you can make  use of  egg cartons they really do work and with  some creativity you can  make something pretty rad and if you're  only doing audio i know that the sounds  and  looks ridiculous but you can make use of  a blanket to cover the camera  dead in the space and create really  crisp audio  the truth is that the quality of your  audio is greatly determined on the type  of microphone you use  for recording high quality audios to  choose the right microphone most people  make the mistake of relying solely on  the built-in microphone on their camera  but i'd suggest you invest in an  external microphone like one of the  following dependents on the kind of film  you want to make  the desktop microphone which sounds  something like this  which you can use for shooting podcasts  voice overs  one location gaming or news channels  lipo microphones there's many different  options of these i'm currently using the  small one these are what the older ones  look like  and they also make one for your cell  phone lipo microphones are great for  shooting talking headshots on locations  that may be loud as they're very  directional and  yeah i just love them they are great a  simple shotgun microphone which is what  i use mainly  it's great for ambience for shooting on  location  it's the most common filmmakers  microphone but you do need to be  vigilant when it comes to noises you  can't control like wind  when shooting with this microphone make  sure that you turn down what's known as  the preamp which is the internal sound  within your camera  to as low as possible